block(r'\section{Decomposition: estimation and numerical diagnostics}',r'\end{document}',r'''\section{Decomposition and held-out-card checks}\label{app:prediction}
\subsection{Sensitivity structure and estimation}
The decomposition separates overall harshness from differences in teachers' responses to individual attributes. A sensitivity variance measures how much teachers differ in the response to one attribute; a covariance measures whether teachers with a stronger response to one attribute tend also to respond more strongly, or less strongly, to another. The diagonal model estimates six sensitivity variances and treats their covariances as zero. The three-pair model adds covariances for gender--background, failures--competences and absence--discipline. The unrestricted model estimates all fifteen pairwise covariances, giving 21 parameters for this part of the model.

All three specifications use the same 17,312 decisions, fixed predictors and 2,164 teachers, with harshness referenced to the Main sample. The fixed design has 34 full-rank columns, and the covariance design distinguishes each proposed component from the residual variance. Two optimizers give closely matching objectives and allocations. The diagonal estimate is interior with a small scaled gradient and a positive-definite Hessian. The correlated models have lower REML AIC, indicating better working-model fit, and reach a covariance boundary: some combination of sensitivities has estimated variance close to zero. The diagonal is retained as the simple primary approximation under the adopted selection rule. Table~\ref{tab:covariance} shows that every aggregate share differs by less than one percentage point across these alternatives.
\inputtab{reused}{covariance_audit}{Covariance structures and model-implied variance allocation}{tab:covariance}

For uncertainty, we resample the 2,520 Main teachers, keeping each teacher's eight cards together and assigning sampled copies separate cluster identifiers. Profile references and harshness are rebuilt in the complete resampled population before selecting records with complete covariates. Attribute centering and the Shapley projections are recalculated in each replicate. The diagonal structure is held fixed, and the SE of each share is its dispersion across 199 resamples. Each fit checks the optimizer status, scaled gradient and Hessian, with a second optimizer used when needed. All 199 fits passed these numerical checks; BOOTBOUNDARY had a boundary variance estimate and were retained. These SE describe sampling dispersion conditional on the chosen model and reconstruction.

The linear model represents variation on the response scale. Approximately OUTSIDE percent of its conditional fitted values fall outside $[0,1]$, so individual probability forecasts are evaluated separately using the logistic models below. Harshness enters with a coefficient of approximately HBCOEF. Because this regressor is constructed from the responses, the allocation uses the model-implied denominator in Equation~\ref{eq:variance_total}.

\subsection{Prediction algorithm and uncertainty}
The primary prediction exercise uses the 2,520 Main teachers and five external folds assigned by teacher. Within teacher, four cards form a randomly selected history and four are reserved for evaluation. For each external fold, the reference probability for profile $x$ is $(r_x+1)/(n_x+2)$ using training teachers. References used for a training teacher exclude that teacher's own responses. The history summary is the average of $Y_{jx}-p_{x,-j}^{\mathrm{train}}$ over the four history cards.

Two logistic calibrations use the profile log-odds as an offset and the same ten treatment-cell, card-set and educational-stage controls. The second adds history. Parameters are fitted to the reserved-card subset of training teachers, and performance is evaluated on reserved cards of teachers in the external fold. For these external teachers, their history affects only their own history summary. Brier and log losses are first averaged within teacher and then equally across teachers. In the matched extension, both calibrations also include experience, teacher gender, age, ownership, permanent public appointment, teaching groups, perceived impact, empathy, effort attribution and resource agreement, using the same 2,164 complete teachers. Educational stage enters once. Continuous covariates are centered and scaled within the training fold in each fit.

The paired teacher bootstrap uses 399 complete reestimations of references, histories and calibrations. Copies retain the original teacher's fold, and all copies of the focal teacher are excluded when constructing that teacher's training references. Uncertainty is conditional on the fixed folds and history partition. Twenty prespecified random partitions assess sensitivity to which cards enter history; the primary Brier reduction ranges from 0.01404 to 0.01613. The ordered check uses the first four cards to anticipate the last four within the same survey encounter. Table~\ref{tab:prediction} includes the no-screen sensitivity and the no-shared-field sensitivity, the latter retaining teachers with at least four nonshared responses and splitting $\lfloor n_j/2\rfloor$ into history. These restrictions change the population or history length. The matched comparison retains its own common cohort and identical splits for its two predictors.
\inputtab{reused}{prediction_validation}{Held-out-card prediction and sensitivity comparisons}{tab:prediction}'''.replace('BOOTBOUNDARY',str(boot['singular'])).replace('OUTSIDE',f"{100*json.loads((O/'Main/decomposition_diagnostics.json').read_text())['outside01']:.1f}").replace('HBCOEF',f"{json.loads((O/'Main/decomposition_diagnostics.json').read_text())['hb']:.3f}"))
